# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: core.autocrlf is true on Windows, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout
# for every text file. A CRLF in a .sh file breaks it on POSIX -- and `bash -n` passes it
# silently, so the syntax gate cannot catch that class. Since the .sh scripts are now part of a
# gating validation path, the risk stopped being cosmetic.
#
# This policy agrees with .editorconfig everywhere the two overlap:
#   .editorconfig  [*] lf  ·  [*.{ps1,psm1,psd1}] crlf  ·  [*.sh] lf
# Keep them in step. If you change one, change the other in the same commit.

* text=auto

# POSIX-executed or machine-parsed: must stay LF.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.version  text eol=lf
*.gitkeep  text eol=lf

# Windows-executed: must stay CRLF.
*.ps1      text eol=crlf
*.psm1     text eol=crlf
*.psd1     text eol=crlf
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.cmd      text eol=crlf

# Paths no extension rule above reaches. These matched `* text=auto` alone, which pins the content
# but not the line ending: `git archive` then converts using the BUILDER's platform, so one tag
# produced CRLF on Windows and LF on POSIX and shipped two different artifacts (v1.15.6: 282,144
# vs 281,685 bytes, five entries, identical once CR is stripped). Nothing else in the tree drifted
# -- these were the only five of 215 tracked files with no explicit eol. Pinning them to lf states
# what .editorconfig `[*] end_of_line = lf` already decided; the stored blobs are LF already, so
# this changes no committed byte, it only takes the platform out of the answer.
# The one *.example today is a POSIX settings file, which must be lf to be usable.
LICENSE        text eol=lf
.editorconfig  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
*.example      text eol=lf
